digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56873-z
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper motivates developing and evaluating P33, an APR-derived antibacterial peptide from conserved or homologous bacterial protein regions, as a broad-spectrum strategy against ESKAPEE/ESKAPE pathogens by testing whether multi-protein co-aggregation can disrupt proteostasis and reduce resistance emergence.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) APR-derived peptide strategies that plausibly induce multi-protein aggregation can be investigated as broad-spectrum antibacterial approaches when conventional resistance-linked threats create an urgent need for new antibiotics."];
  "Node_1" [label="(3,2,0) These six pathogens were linked to an estimated 3.57 million deaths related to antibiotic resistance in 2019."];
  "Node_11" [label="(22,2,0) P33 was previously identified in a screen against E. coli using randomly selected APRs of E. coli proteins."];
  "Node_12" [label="(23,2,0) The screen was designed to select APRs with a maximal number of homologous fragments in different proteins across the E. coli proteome."];
  "Node_14" [label="(0,0,0) If targeting multiple APRs causes widespread aggregation, then this strategy is a valid template for peptide design."];
  "Node_18" [label="(45,3,0) P33 showed broad-spectrum activity with a conserved mode of action across the tested spectrum."];
  "Node_2" [label="(4,1,0) ESKAPEE refers to a group of highly virulent and antibiotic-resistant bacterial pathogens."];
  "Node_22" [label="(0,0,0) If P33's broad-spectrum efficacy is driven by its multi-target aggregation mechanism, then a lack of resistance build-up will be observed."];
  "Node_3" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given ESKAPEE pathogens are highly virulent and linked to millions of deaths, therefore they represent a severe global health threat."];
  "Node_4" [label="(0,0,0) If a pathogen group represents a severe global health threat, then new approaches to develop effective antibiotics against them are urgently needed."];
  "Node_5" [label="(5,1,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the scale of the threat they pose, therefore new approaches to develop effective antibiotics against drug-resistant pathogens are urgently needed."];
  "Node_6" [label="(13,2,0) The novel mechanism of action involves inducing aggregation of a target protein, resulting in loss of function."];
  "Node_7" [label="(14,2,0) APRs can drive the misfolding and aggregation of proteins containing homologous APRs."];
  "Node_9" [label="(0,0,0) If targeting APRs is an effective mechanism, then targeting APRs that occur in multiple proteins will cause widespread aggregation and proteostatic collapse."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The urgent need for new approaches against drug-resistant ESKAPEE pathogens, together with the predicted resistance-refractory implications of P33's putative multi-target aggregation mechanism, supports evaluating P33 as a potential novel antibacterial approach."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because induced aggregation can inactivate target proteins and homologous aggregation-prone regions can promote aggregation among proteins containing them, APRs provide a plausible mechanism for disrupting bacterial protein function through aggregation."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) If APR targeting is applied to homologous APRs present across multiple proteins, the strategy is expected to cause widespread aggregation and may contribute to proteostatic collapse."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) P33 was identified as an APR-derived peptide from a screen intended to enrich APRs with many homologous fragments across the E. coli proteome, rather than being proven to have been deliberately designed to target multiple APRs simultaneously."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) A strategy that targets homologous APRs across multiple proteins is a reasonable template for peptide design expected to aggregate many proteins."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) P33's origin from a screen enriched for APRs with many homologous proteome fragments makes it a plausible candidate for multi-protein co-aggregation, but does not by itself prove a specific bias toward transmembrane or ribosomal proteins."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) P33's broad-spectrum activity with a conserved mode of action is plausibly explained by an APR-homology-mediated, multi-protein aggregation mechanism."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Under the explanation that P33's broad-spectrum efficacy is driven by a multi-target aggregation mechanism, a lack of resistance build-up would be the expected observation."];
  "Node_1" -> "Node_3" [label="induction-case"];
